.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt RT_SIGQUEUEINFO 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rt_sigqueueinfo ,
.Nm rt_tgsigqueueinfo
.Nd send a signal with its siginfo
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ft int
.Fn rt_sigqueueinfo "pid_t pid" "int sig" "siginfo_t *info"
.Ft int
.Fn rt_tgsigqueueinfo "pid_t tgid" "pid_t tid" "int sig" "siginfo_t *info"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn rt_sigqueueinfo ,
number 178 on i386 and 129 on x86-64, which the C libraries'
.Fn sigqueue
calls, sends
.Fa sig
to the process
.Fa pid
with the code and the fields of
.Fa info ,
which a handler,
.Xr sigtimedwait 2
and
.Xr signalfd 2
are given.
The fields are read by the layout Linux's
.Fn siginfo_layout
gives
.Fa sig
and the code, whatever
.Fa si_signo
says, as Linux sets it to
.Fa sig
first: a sender's pid and uid; those and a value; a timer's id, overrun
and value; a band and a descriptor; a child's pid, uid, status and times;
a fault's address, with the lower and upper bounds of
.Dv SEGV_BNDERR ,
the key of
.Dv SEGV_PKUERR ,
the address bits of
.Dv BUS_MCEERR_AR
and
.Dv BUS_MCEERR_AO ,
or the data, type and flags of
.Dv TRAP_PERF ;
or a seccomp
.Dv SIGSYS Ns 's
call address, number and architecture.
A code above 0 and below
.Dv SI_KERNEL
past the last of its signal's own codes has a band and a descriptor up to
6 and a sender's pid and uid above.
A native caller's siginfo keeps every byte of its fields, as Linux keeps
it, and a native receiver gets them all back; an i386 caller's on x86-64 keeps the
fields of its layout alone, and an i386 receiver gets those fields with
pointers and values cut to 32 bits.
.Fn rt_tgsigqueueinfo ,
number 335 on i386 and 297 on x86-64, sends it to the thread
.Fa tid
of the process
.Fa tgid
alone, as
.Xr tgkill 2
does.
The permission is that of
.Xr kill 2 .
A code of zero or above, or
.Dv SI_TKILL ,
is the kernel's or
.Xr tgkill 2 Ns 's ,
and a caller may give it only to its own thread, named by
.Fa pid
or
.Fa tid ,
as on Linux, which checks that before it looks for the target.
A native caller's code whose layout Linux does not know, one not listed
above, from
.Dv SI_USER
down to
.Dv SI_DETHREAD ,
.Dv SI_ASYNCNL
or
.Dv SI_KERNEL ,
must leave zero every byte of
.Fa info
past the fields the kernel keeps, 48 on x86-64 and 32 on i386, since no
receiver could get them back.
A signal of 0 checks the target and sends nothing.
The kernel copies the whole siginfo before resolving the target; a page
fault during that copy may let another process reap the target.
.Pp
The kernel keeps one pending instance of each signal, so a real-time
signal sent again while it is pending is merged with it, where Linux
queues each.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er E2BIG
A native caller gave a code of a layout Linux does not know with a byte
past the kept fields that is not zero.
.It Er EFAULT
.Fa info
is not in the caller's memory.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa sig
is outside 0 to 64, or for
.Fn rt_tgsigqueueinfo
an id is not positive.
.It Er EPERM
The caller may not signal the target, or gave a kernel's code to a thread
other than itself.
.It Er ESRCH
No process or thread has the id, or the thread is not one of
.Fa tgid Ns 's .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr tgkill 2
